.TH "SKARN-ASSESS" "1" "2026-06-23" "skarn 0.38.0" "Skarn Manual"
.SH NAME
skarn\-assess \- Scan every AI session, print a redacted summary. Free, no
license
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBskarn\fR \fBassess\fR [options]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Scan every AI session on this machine and print a friendly risk summary, with
an optional shareable redacted report and a scoped incident dossier. Outside a
dossier it also lists any credential saved in an assistant's permission rules,
which it reads on this machine from the configuration files that skarn vet
\-\-all\-projects reads. Free, no config, no license on this machine. Reading
a copied home with \-\-evidence\-home requires Pro, and assess then reads
saved permission rules from the copied home's own configuration files. Run at
a terminal without \-\-evidence\-home it also names, once per release, a hook
in your own per\-user assistant config that has fallen behind this build, so
an event a newer template gates does not go unscanned unnoticed; it looks at
that config only, never at a repo's committed hooks and never at a
fleet\-managed one.
.PP
The zero\-config machine\-scan wedge: scan every AI coding session on the
machine and print a friendly risk summary, no flags and no license. Reading a
copied home with \-\-evidence\-home requires Pro. \-o writes a shareable
redacted report (HTML or Markdown); \-\-json emits the redacted scan for
scripting. The scan is scoped by the same filters as check. The Findings line
says how many critical findings are attack chains or behaviors with no secret
value, so a reader knows why the exposures list below can hold fewer rows than
the critical count. The summary, the Markdown report and the HTML report show
one row per distinct secret value per session file, naming the other rules
that matched it after also matched; \-\-json keeps every incident and marks
the primary with also_matched, a member with grouped_under, and
stats.distinct_secret_values with the number of primaries, one per distinct
value per session file. Assess also lists the credentials it detects in saved
assistant permission rules, including Gemini CLI's user policy files and Codex
CLI's rules files, and in VS Code Copilot Chat's terminal auto\-approve rules,
the finding skarn vet reports as vet\-permission\-plaintext\-credential, for
every assistant that rule covers, under the heading Credentials in saved
permission rules. Without \-\-evidence\-home, it reads the configuration files
skarn vet \-\-all\-projects reads: the home layer, the system managed layer,
VS Code's user directory (its rows carry the anchor vscode_user in \-\-json
and the location VS Code user followed by the file in the reports), the
project at the working directory, and every project Claude Code records in
~/.claude.json and in the .claude.json of each directory named by
CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR or SKARN_CLAUDE_DIRS, under the same root variables vet
follows. Gemini CLI's user settings row carries the anchor gemini_home and the
relative path settings.json in \-\-json. The reports show gemini home
settings.json for that row and gemini home policies/ followed by the file name
for a user policy. A credential from Codex CLI's user rules files carries the
anchor codex_home and a path relative to that directory, such as
rules/default.rules, in \-\-json, and the reports show codex home followed by
that path. A project Claude Code never recorded is read only when assess
starts in it, so the section can differ by start directory. The section stays
outside the risk score, the severity and finding counts, the risk band and the
exit code: a configuration file that could not be read or was not fully
scanned is a gap named inside the section, never an exit 6, and never part of
the session coverage sentence. On a live home, the terminal summary gives the
section one line, after the next\-step lines and before the share line, and
only when the section lists a credential or did not complete; the line counts
the rules and the files that carry them and names skarn assess \-o report.html
as the place that lists every one. \-\-cli keeps the rows of the assistants
that map to that tool, and every row is high, so \-\-severity low, medium or
high keeps them all. \-\-project, \-\-session, \-\-user and \-\-severity
critical leave the section out of scope, and assess reads no assistant
configuration file for it; \-\-hours and \-\-baseline do not change it. With
\-\-evidence\-home, the same filters apply. When the section is in scope,
assess checks the copied home's own configuration. An existing directory typed
in a refused home spelling produces one gap at the home, and assess reads no
configuration there. Even when the section lists no credential, the terminal
summary and the Markdown and HTML reports include the sentence `Read from the
evidence home's own configuration files; project files and the system layer
were not read.` Each half of the output is masked with what the other half
detected: the section with every value the session scan detected, including
values below the requested severity, and the session fields, such as contexts
and project names, with every value vet detected in configuration. For a VS
Code terminal auto\-approve key, the session fields are also masked with the
regular\-expression\-escaped spelling VS Code saves the value in and with the
whole key as JSON decodes it, slashes and anchors included; a transcript that
quotes a settings file's own bytes in another spelling, such as a JSON escape,
is not matched. A detected value shorter than 16 bytes, or its escaped
spelling, that a session field writes directly between letters or digits is
not masked there. Assess counts a link on a session store path that Skarn does
not follow as check does, prints its lines on stderr after the summary or the
\-\-json output, and lists them in the Markdown and HTML reports, the
\-\-dossier ones included.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-o\fR, \fB\-\-out\fR \fIfile\fR
Write a self\-contained, redacted share report to this file; .md or .markdown
renders Markdown, any other extension renders HTML. The report names a session
file by its basename and a project by its display name, and writes a home
directory inside a code\-context snippet as ~. The Markdown report ends each
exposure line with the finding's confidence and adds likely test value when
that flag is set, then the reach as the number of sessions and assistants when
the value appeared in more than one session, and the execution when the
transcript records the content written or executed; the HTML report shows all
of them in the finding drawer. Both reports carry the Act on this first
section that the check text format prints, and its line of deprioritized
values; in the HTML report each row opens its finding drawer, and selecting a
tool, a project or a user hides the section. When the Credentials in saved
permission rules section prints, it comes before every other section: in the
Markdown report right after the header lines, and in the HTML report as a
static block above the Security view; otherwise both reports open with the Act
on this first section. That section names no absolute path. It shows a file in
the home directory as ~/ followed by its path relative to the home directory,
a project file as the project's display name, which is the last component of
its root, followed by its relative path, a system managed file by its system
path, never a staged root, and a file in another assistant home as the
assistant, the word home and the relative path. A locator in ~/.claude.json
that names a project shows only the last component of the project key, and is
withheld when a detected value reaches into that component; any other locator
that names a path or carries a masked value is withheld. When that section
prints anything, a report with no session finding says No leaked credentials
or attack patterns were found in AI coding sessions, without the word Clean.
The file is written atomically as a new owner\-only file on macOS and Linux
(on Windows it takes the permissions of its directory): a regular file at the
path, or a symlink to one, is replaced by the new file; a device or FIFO at
the path is written in place and keeps its own permissions, and so is a
symlink to one of those or to a process stream such as /dev/stdout; a
directory at the path is refused.
.TP
\fB\-\-json\fR
Emit the redacted scan as JSON for scripting, instead of the friendly summary:
the shape of the serve /api/scan view followed by a saved_approvals key, which
/api/scan never carries. Each incident carries confidence and
likely_test_value, the same values `skarn check \-\-format json` reports as
confidence and likelyTestValue, and a reach object with sessions, assistants,
assistant_names, projects, chains, findings, suppressed_findings and
execution, the counts `skarn check \-\-format json` reports as reach, never
with project names. A ranked incident carries triage_rank (1 to 5),
triage_reasons (its confidence, active use, issuer class and attack chain
labels) and triage_seen (the YYYY\-MM\-DD date its recency was ranked on), and
stats carries triage_more and the three deprioritized counts
deprioritized_likely_test_value, deprioritized_low_confidence and
deprioritized_suppressed. Every other incident carries triage_rank null, an
empty triage_reasons array and an empty triage_seen. A dossier, and an
/api/scan response filtered by cli, project or user, carries every incident at
those defaults and the four counts at 0, so no section renders there. With
\-\-dossier the body is the dossier envelope that wraps this scan, not the
bare scan shape, and it carries no saved_approvals key. Outside a dossier,
saved_approvals is the last key and always carries six keys: state (checked,
failed, or out_of_scope), reason (a string when state is failed, null
otherwise), projects_checked, projects_missing, findings and unreadable. With
\-\-evidence\-home, an in\-scope check of saved permission rules reads the
copied home's own configuration, sets state to checked and projects_checked to
0. Each finding carries rule, severity, assistant, anchor, config_path,
relative_path, project_root, project, locator and detail, where config_path
and project_root keep the masked absolute paths that skarn vet \-\-format json
reports; each unreadable entry carries path and reason. The scan fields are
masked with the values vet detected in configuration, as the reports are.
.TP
\fB\-\-offline\fR
Disable all network access. Assess is already egress\-free; this is the
belt\-and\-suspenders guarantee.
.TP
\fB\-\-hours\fR \fIn\fR
Scan window in hours; 0 (the default) scans every session on the machine. The
default is 0.
.TP
\fB\-\-severity\fR \fIlevel\fR
Only summarize findings at or above this severity; the default (low) scans
everything and curates the display. One of: low, medium, high, critical. The
default is low.
.TP
\fB\-\-cli\fR \fIname\fR
Restrict the scan to one assistant's sessions. If this machine has no tool by
that name, skarn exits 6 rather than scanning nothing. A custom tool declared
in ~/.skarn.json is accepted by its configured name. One of: claude, gemini,
codex, cursor, copilot, kimi, grok, grokbot, antigravity, opencode, cline,
copilot\-code, kiro.
.TP
\fB\-\-project\fR \fIpath\fR
Restrict the scan to matching projects: a comma\-separated list of names or
paths, matched case\-insensitively against a session's project display name,
its canonical root path, or a parent directory of that root. A session read
from a Compliance API export carries no project, so any \-\-project value
excludes it.
.TP
\fB\-\-session\fR \fIid\fR
Assess a single session by id.
.TP
\fB\-\-rules\fR \fIpath\fR
Load a custom TOML rules file in addition to the bundled rules. A rule pattern
whose compiled form exceeds 16 KiB is refused like any pattern that fails to
compile, so skarn exits 6 without scanning; an allowlist pattern over that
bound is skipped, so its suppression does not apply. The Credentials in saved
permission rules section uses the same rules as the session scan: the custom
file in place of the bundled community rules that skarn vet runs, plus the
AI\-specific bundled rules, so assess \-\-rules can list a credential that
skarn vet does not and can miss one that skarn vet lists.
.TP
\fB\-\-baseline\fR \fIpath\fR
Suppress findings recorded in this accepted\-findings baseline. Without this
flag, ~/.config/skarn/baseline.json is applied automatically when it exists. A
directory of baselines requires a Pro license. Without \-\-evidence\-home,
assess warns about an unlicensed directory and scans without suppression.
Reading a copied home with \-\-evidence\-home requires Pro. With
\-\-evidence\-home, Skarn skips the personal default baseline and applies only
an explicit \-\-baseline.
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-color\fR
Disable colored output.
.TP
\fB\-\-dossier\fR \fIselector\fR
Scope \-o and \-\-json to one incident dossier: one finding by its
64\-character fingerprint, or one session by id, auto\-detected by shape. A
reused credential matches every session it appears in. The dossier holds the
redacted facts Skarn has for the selection, plus an explicit list of the CRA
Article 14 reporting fields Skarn cannot supply. It is a local share artifact;
Skarn does not file reports or determine severity. Every format names a
project by its display name. The HTML and JSON dossiers name a session file by
its basename, write a home directory inside a code\-context snippet as ~, and
leave out the project root and the user and workspace ids. The Markdown
dossier prints none of those fields except the basename of a selected session
file that held a record the scan could not parse, and it states the coverage
counters of the whole scan the dossier was drawn from. The HTML dossier shows
the coverage warning of the HTML report whenever that scan was incomplete, and
its compliance export notice whenever that scan withheld export sessions. A
dossier carries no Credentials in saved permission rules section and no
saved_approvals key: assess collects nothing for it.
.TP
\fB\-\-user\fR \fIname\fR
Restrict the scan to matching users: a comma\-separated list of user ids or
email addresses, where an id matches exactly and an email address matches
case\-insensitively. Only a session read from a Compliance API export carries
either, so on a machine without an export, or without the Enterprise license
that reading one needs, every \-\-user value matches nothing.
.TP
\fB\-\-evidence\-home\fR \fIdir\fR
Read a copied home's default session stores (Pro). For configuration
discovery, Skarn reads the copied home's own assistant configuration. Skarn
looks where this machine's operating system keeps each store, so a copy taken
on another operating system can show fewer session or configuration rows. The
Compliance API export root resolves under dir too, and reading it still needs
Enterprise. Session discovery ignores store overrides, XDG_DATA_HOME,
XDG_CONFIG_HOME and SKARN_COMPLIANCE_EXPORT_DIR. The license, EULA record,
~/.skarn.json, caches and outputs stay with the examiner. Skarn ignores the
copied .skarn.json. Skarn resolves ~/ in sessions_path under dir and reports
skipped absolute paths. All history is in scope unless you specify \-\-hours,
\-\-since or \-\-until. Only an explicit \-\-baseline applies. Assess reads
the copied home's saved permission rules for saved_approvals when in scope.
Vet reads only home\-layer configuration and refuses \-\-all\-projects and
\-\-project\-only. Inventory reads sessions and home\-layer configuration. Its
\-\-project filter applies only to sessions. Check writes the same
skarn.inventory line with \-\-format ndjson and refuses \-\-drop,
\-\-audit\-verify and \-\-baseline\-merge. Restore omits cd and names the
evidence machine's unchecked directory on stderr. The session scan skips
transcript path checks, reads derived metadata only under dir and follows no
link below the evidence home: each refused link counts once as an unreadable
store, and one stderr line per reason gives the count. Configuration discovery
in vet and inventory follows no symbolic link below dir either. Both accept a
plain UNC share as the evidence home and exit 6 for a refused network or
device spelling or a control character. Skarn refuses outputs under dir and
homes that contain the examiner's home or overlap Skarn's config or cache
directories (exit 1). Missing, unreadable or non\-directory paths, paths
containing *, and paths that pass through a link to a network path, a link
Skarn cannot resolve or a component Skarn cannot check for links cause exit 6;
the stderr line names the link. Licensing failures exit 7 (no usable license)
or 5 (insufficient tier) before evidence access. Input files can reside
anywhere. JSON, SARIF, NDJSON and the audit record have no dedicated
evidence\-home field. Finding paths show the directory. The evidence pack
names it on one line. For check, \-\-fail\-on\-scan\-error fails incomplete
scans. Requires a Pro license.
.SH FILES
.TP
\fB~/.claude/settings.json, ~/.claude/settings.local.json, ./.claude/settings.json, ./.claude/settings.local.json\fR
Claude Code hooks and permission rules (allow, ask, deny). Read, never
written. Vet reads the project\-local pair at the working directory, the git
top and, in a linked worktree, the main checkout. Assess reads the
project\-local pair in the working directory's project and in every project
Claude Code records.
.TP
\fB~/.claude.json, ~/.mcp.json, ./.mcp.json\fR
Claude Code MCP server definitions and the legacy per\-project allowedTools
lists in ~/.claude.json. Read, never written. Vet reads the project\-local
file at the working directory, the git top and, in a linked worktree, the main
checkout. Assess reads the project\-local file in the working directory's
project and in every project Claude Code records. The Copilot CLI also loads a
project .mcp.json, so vet reads its bare entries as Copilot CLI servers under
the Copilot CLI's own source row: each top\-level key other than mcpServers
whose value is an object, and mcpServers itself when that object holds a
command, url, httpUrl, serverUrl or endpoint string directly; the servers
inside mcpServers stay Claude Code's. ~/.mcp.json has no Copilot CLI row of
its own, but when the home directory is itself a visited project, vet reads
the .mcp.json there as that project's file under both rows, as the Copilot CLI
loads a .mcp.json from the working directory. A container server's locator
starts mcpServers.<name> and a bare server's starts <name>, so the two can
coincide (a container server a and a bare server named mcpServers.a), and a
finding does not say which reading produced it. \-\-all\-projects reads the
projects keys in ~/.claude.json and also in $CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR/.claude.json
and each $SKARN_CLAUDE_DIRS entry's .claude.json; Skarn does not vet those
declared directories' other content.
.TP
\fB~/.claude/plugins/, ~/.claude/skills/\fR
Installed Claude Code plugins and skills. Vet reads each plugin.json and
skill.json manifest for its install source and integrity marker.
.TP
\fB~/.codex/config.toml, ~/.codex/hooks.json\fR
Codex CLI MCP server definitions and hooks. Read by vet through a scoped
reader that keeps only the launcher command, its arguments, and the endpoint
url. When CODEX_HOME is nonempty, vet, assess, inventory and the MCP
vet_configs tool read $CODEX_HOME/config.toml and $CODEX_HOME/hooks.json
instead, with no fallback to ~/.codex; setup and doctor still read ~/.codex.
.TP
\fB~/.cursor/mcp.json, ~/.cursor/hooks.json\fR
Cursor MCP server definitions and hooks. Read by vet.
.TP
\fB~/.copilot/mcp\-config.json, ~/.copilot/config.json, ~/.copilot/settings.json\fR
Copilot CLI MCP server definitions, application state, and user settings, all
resolved under $COPILOT_HOME when it is set, which replaces the default root
rather than layering over it. A declared root holding no Copilot store reads
exactly as an absent default one does: installation is decided by a config
file that was actually read, or by the session\-state and hooks markers under
whichever root is in force, never by the root's own existence. Read by vet.
From settings.json vet resolves whether CLI sessions are exported to GitHub.
remoteExport: false is the opt\-out GitHub documents as the way to stop
session syncing, remoteSessions: true forces export back on regardless of it,
and the documented default with neither set is sync on. remote is recorded as
a contributing key but never decides, because GitHub documents it two ways
that do not agree: the settings table says off keeps session data local only,
while the page on session syncing names remoteExport as the opt\-out and does
not mention remote at all. A config carrying only remote: off is therefore
reported as exporting, and the finding lists every key that was set, so a
reader who has decided the settings table is authoritative can see exactly
what skarn read and dismiss it. Where no settings.json exists at all, which is
the state a fresh install is in since neither the CLI nor its
\-\-no\-remote\-export flag writes one, the documented default is reported
against the path the file would occupy, so an untouched machine is not
mistaken for an opted\-out one. Comments are tolerated, since GitHub documents
JSONC for this file and the CLI writes a comment header into config.json
beside it. Read\-only; never written.
.TP
\fB~/.scout/m\-settings.json\fR
Microsoft Scout's permission grants, auto\-approve settings, and experiment
flags. Read by vet. The path has no platform branch. Scout resolves its config
root to ~/.scout on macOS and Windows alike.
.TP
\fB~/.scout/m\-mcp\-servers.json\fR
Microsoft Scout MCP server definitions. Read by vet. A user\-added server
nests its launcher or endpoint under a config object; a built\-in server has
none, because Scout generates its command at run time.
.TP
\fB~/.copilot/data.db\fR
The Copilot CLI's SQLite state store, which holds the signed\-in GitHub
account's access token and any bring\-your\-own\-key provider headers
alongside projects, workflows and review comments. Vet reads this file's POSIX
mode by a non\-following stat and never opens it: no byte of its contents is
read, the database is never opened as a database, so no token and no header
value can reach a finding and the only thing reported is whether the mode
grants group or other read; a symlinked store is reported as a coverage gap
rather than measured through its target, and a store whose owner\-read bit is
off is still measured. Its presence marks ~/.copilot as a live Copilot CLI
install for the Microsoft Scout residue rule, the same way session\-state/ and
hooks/ do, so the row is never skipped as residue. Never a scan source: the
session commands do not read it.
.TP
\fB~/Library/Application Support/Grok Bot/sand\-secrets.json\fR
Grok Bot's plaintext third\-party credential store, on macOS. Grok Bot is
xAI's always\-on desktop agents app, a different product from the Grok Build
CLI. Vet reads this file's POSIX mode by a non\-following stat and never opens
it: not one byte of its contents is read and the store is never parsed, so no
key name and no value from it can reach a finding and the only thing reported
is whether the mode grants group or other read; a symlinked store is reported
as a coverage gap rather than measured through its target. The path carries no
platform branch, so the store is vetted on macOS only; the Linux and Windows
locations are not read.
.TP
\fB~/.grokbot/settings.json\fR
Grok Bot's local\-exec daemon settings. Vet reads two declared posture flags
from this file, egressTunnelEnabled and webauthnProxyEnabled, and records only
whether each is present and true. A flag that is absent or false raises
nothing, so a default install stays silent.
.TP
\fB~/.grokbot/local\-exec\-daemon\-connection.json\fR
Grok Bot's local\-exec daemon connection state: the gateway base URL and the
bearer, network and VNC proxy tokens the cloud side uses to execute on this
machine. Vet treats the file as a presence signal and never parses it, so the
gateway URL and every token stay out of the process as well as out of every
output format. Its presence declares that the channel is configured, not that
a daemon is running.
.TP
\fB~/.gemini/settings.json\fR
Gemini CLI's user settings: MCP server definitions read by vet, and the hooks
object skarn setup \-\-agent gemini merges the guard into and doctor reads as
the user precedence layer. When GEMINI_CLI_HOME is nonempty, vet, assess,
inventory, and the MCP vet_configs tool read
$GEMINI_CLI_HOME/.gemini/settings.json instead. Setup and doctor still use
~/.gemini/settings.json.
.TP
\fB~/.grok/config.toml, ~/.grok/managed_config.toml, ~/.grok/requirements.toml\fR
Grok Build's user, managed, and requirements configuration layers. Read by vet
through a scoped TOML reader that collects MCP server launchers and endpoints,
the variable NAMES (never the values) of an MCP env table, the [ui]
permission_mode and yolo settings, allow\-action [permission] rules in both
the compact string\-array and the structured rules forms, hook handlers in
both TOML notations, and [[ui.notifications.hooks]] commands. Relocate the
whole directory with $GROK_HOME. Syntax the reader does not model, such as a
multiline string, is reported as a coverage gap naming the file and line
rather than skipped silently.
.TP
\fB/etc/grok/managed_config.toml, /etc/grok/requirements.toml\fR
Grok Build's system\-wide managed and requirements layers, deployed by an
organization. Read by vet exactly as the user layers are; SKARN_MANAGED_ROOT
prefixes both paths, so a staged payload can be vetted before it reaches a
machine.
.TP
\fB~/.grok/hooks/, ~/.grok/hooks\-paths\fR
Grok Build's global JSON hook directory and the registry file naming extra
hook sources. Vet reads the .json files one level down in the directory,
regular files only, and loads each ABSOLUTE line of the registry as a further
hook source; relative lines are ignored the way Grok ignores them, and a UNC
or //host/share target is recorded as a coverage gap and never opened. Both
are bounded per directory and per registry, and exceeding a bound is reported
rather than silently truncated.
.TP
\fB./.grok/config.toml, ./.grok/hooks/\fR
Grok Build's project\-scoped configuration and hook directory, contributing
MCP servers, plugins, and permission rules. Vet reads them at the working
directory, the git top and, in a linked worktree, the main checkout, while
Grok itself discovers a .grok/config.toml at every level from the repository
root down. Project hooks are collected whatever the folder\-trust state,
because a declared hook becomes live the moment the folder is trusted, with no
configuration change. Assess reads them in the working directory's project and
in every project Claude Code records.
.TP
\fB%LOCALAPPDATA%\eMicrosoft\eCopilot\eUser Data\eCopilotCodingHome\e<profile>\emcp\-config.json, %LOCALAPPDATA%\eMicrosoft\eCopilot\eUser Data\eCopilotCodingHome\e<profile>\econfig.json, %LOCALAPPDATA%\eMicrosoft\eCopilot\eUser Data\eCopilotCodingHome\e<profile>\esettings.json (Windows)\fR
The configuration files of one Copilot in Code profile, read by vet. A profile
is a directory inside either CopilotCodingHome directory of %LOCALAPPDATA%,
the MSIX twin included, or on macOS inside ~/Library/Application
Support/Microsoft/CopilotAppHost/CopilotCodingHome/, that holds
session\-state, hooks or data.db, plus a $COPILOT_HOME that names a directory
inside a CopilotCodingHome directory. On Linux only that $COPILOT_HOME reaches
a profile. Such a $COPILOT_HOME is never the Copilot CLI root, so the Copilot
CLI rows read ~/.copilot instead. mcp\-config.json is the file the Copilot CLI
documents under $COPILOT_HOME, and a profile is Code's $COPILOT_HOME; the file
was absent on the verified install and the survey did not establish whether
Code reads it, so vet reports it as declared configuration only when it
exists. config.json is read with the comment header the app writes. From
settings.json vet reports only an export the file declares, remoteSessions:
true. The sync default GitHub documents is the Copilot CLI's, so a profile
with no settings.json, or one that sets neither remoteSessions nor
remoteExport, raises no finding. Vet does not read a profile's
installed\-plugins or extensions folder, a repository's .github\eextensions
folder, the Copilot app's own MCP configuration or the project folders under
the Copilot projects root, because neither their layout nor their file names
were measured. A profile folder that exists but cannot be opened is a coverage
gap. A CopilotCodingHome directory, or a $COPILOT_HOME profile, reached
through a link whose target is a network path is not opened and is a coverage
gap named NetworkPathRefused. Read\-only; never written. Verified against
Copilot app `156.0.4314.58` with GitHub Copilot CLI `1.0.93`.
.TP
\fB%LOCALAPPDATA%\eMicrosoft\eCopilot\eUser Data\eCopilotCodingHome\e<profile>\edata.db (Windows)\fR
The SQLite app database of one Copilot in Code profile; its accounts table has
an access_token column. Vet reads this file's POSIX mode by a non\-following
stat and never opens it: no byte of its contents is read, and the only thing
reported is whether the mode grants group or other read. A symlinked store is
reported as a coverage gap rather than measured through its target. Windows
has no POSIX mode, so on Windows vet counts the file as examined and reports
no mode finding. On macOS the finding applies to the data.db of every profile
under ~/Library/Application
Support/Microsoft/CopilotAppHost/CopilotCodingHome/, and on macOS and Linux to
a profile that a $COPILOT_HOME names. Vet reads none of its rows. Verified
against Copilot app `156.0.4314.58`.
.TP
\fB%LOCALAPPDATA%\eMicrosoft\eCopilot\eUser Data\eCopilotCodingHome\e<profile>\ehooks\e (Windows)\fR
The hook folder of one Copilot in Code profile, where skarn setup \-\-agent
copilot\-code writes skarn.json. Vet reads the .json files one level down,
regular files only, under the same bounds as the other hook directories, and
reports each command, bash and powershell field of a hook entry, flat or
nested, at its own locator, for example hooks.preToolUse[0].powershell.
Copilot in Code loaded a hook file from this folder under any file name. When
the folder, or a folder on the way to it, is a link whose target is a network
path, vet opens nothing through it and reports a coverage gap named
NetworkPathRefused on the folder. Verified against Copilot app `156.0.4314.58`
with GitHub Copilot CLI `1.0.93`.
.TP
\fB~/.copilot/hooks/\fR
The Copilot CLI's personal hook folder, under $COPILOT_HOME when it is set and
does not name a Copilot in Code profile. Vet reads the .json files one level
down, regular files only, and reports each command, bash and powershell field
of a hook entry. On a Microsoft Scout machine whose ~/.copilot is only residue
the row is skipped, as the other ~/.copilot rows are.
.TP
\fB./.github/hooks/\fR
The working directory's Copilot hook folder, which the Copilot CLI and the
Copilot cloud agent read, and which Copilot in Code read in a folder project.
Vet reads the .json files one level down, regular files only, at the working
directory, the git top and, in a linked worktree, the main checkout, and
reports each command, bash and powershell field of a hook entry. Vet reads it
whatever the project type; Copilot in Code reading it in a repository project
was not measured. Assess reads it in the working directory's project and in
every project Claude Code records.
.TP
\fB~/.kiro/settings/mcp.json\fR
The Kiro CLI's user MCP configuration. Vet reads MCP server launchers,
endpoints, environment entries, and headers. Relocate with $KIRO_HOME.
.TP
\fB~/.kiro/mcp.json\fR
The home MCP configuration that Kiro CLI 2.x reads. Vet reads MCP server
launchers, endpoints, environment entries, and headers. A probe with Kiro CLI
3.4.0 on macOS found that neither harness read $KIRO_HOME/mcp.json, so vet
reads this file for 2.x installs. Relocate with $KIRO_HOME.
.TP
\fB./.kiro/settings/mcp.json\fR
The Kiro CLI's project MCP configuration. Vet reads MCP server launchers,
endpoints, environment entries, and headers. Assess reads it in the working
directory's project and in every project Claude Code records.
.TP
\fB~/.kiro/hooks/\fR
The Kiro CLI's user standalone JSON hook files. Vet reads hook commands,
including command actions and confirmation commands, directly in this
directory. Relocate with $KIRO_HOME.
.TP
\fB./.kiro/hooks/\fR
The Kiro CLI's project standalone JSON hook files. Vet reads hook commands,
including command actions and confirmation commands, directly in this
directory. Assess reads them in the working directory's project and in every
project Claude Code records.
.TP
\fB~/.kiro/settings/permissions.yaml\fR
The Kiro CLI's user permissions file. Vet reads permission rules and policies,
