\fB./.codex/config.toml, ./.codex/hooks.json\fR
Codex CLI project MCP server definitions and hooks, read by vet at the working
directory, the git top and, in a linked worktree, the main checkout through
the same readers as ~/.codex/config.toml and ~/.codex/hooks.json, whether or
not Codex trusts the project, and reported apart from the home files. Assess
reads them in the working directory's project and in every project Claude Code
records.
.TP
\fB./.cursor/mcp.json, ./.cursor/hooks.json\fR
Cursor project MCP server definitions and hooks, read by vet at the working
directory, the git top and, in a linked worktree, the main checkout, whether
or not the workspace is trusted, and reported apart from ~/.cursor/mcp.json
and ~/.cursor/hooks.json. Assess reads them in the working directory's project
and in every project Claude Code records.
.TP
\fB./.github/mcp.json\fR
The Copilot CLI's project MCP server definitions, read by vet at the working
directory, the git top and, in a linked worktree, the main checkout and
reported apart from ~/.copilot/mcp\-config.json. Both shapes the Copilot CLI
documents for a project file are read: servers under a top\-level mcpServers
object, at locators that start mcpServers.<name>, and the bare form, where
each top\-level key whose value is an object is a server name, at locators
that start <name>. An mcpServers object that itself holds a command, url,
httpUrl, serverUrl or endpoint string is also read as a bare server named
mcpServers, and an mcpServers value that is not an object is a coverage gap
named UnsupportedCopilotMcpShape. Permission grants anywhere in the file
(autoApprove, alwaysAllow and the other grant keys) are read as they are in
~/.copilot/mcp\-config.json. Assess reads it in the working directory's
project and in every project Claude Code records.
.TP
\fB./.gemini/settings.json\fR
Gemini CLI's project settings: MCP server definitions and hooks, read by vet
at the working directory, the git top and, in a linked worktree, the main
checkout whatever the folder\-trust state, because the file is on disk before
the developer decides whether to trust the folder. Reported apart from
~/.gemini/settings.json. Assess reads it in the working directory's project
and in every project Claude Code records.
.TP
\fB~/Library/Application Support/Code/User/settings.json (macOS), ~/.config/Code/User/settings.json (Linux), %APPDATA%\eCode\eUser\esettings.json (Windows), ~/Library/Application Support/Code/User/profiles/<key>/settings.json (macOS), ~/.config/Code/User/profiles/<key>/settings.json (Linux), %APPDATA%\eCode\eUser\eprofiles\e<key>\esettings.json (Windows)\fR
VS Code's user settings and the settings of each VS Code profile, read by vet
and by assess for VS Code Copilot Chat's terminal auto\-approve rules. The
directory follows VS Code's own rule: $VSCODE_APPDATA/Code/User when
VSCODE_APPDATA is set and not empty, on every platform; otherwise
%APPDATA%\eCode\eUser on Windows (falling back to
%USERPROFILE%\eAppData\eRoaming\eCode\eUser), ~/Library/Application
Support/Code/User on macOS, and $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/Code/User on Linux (falling
back to ~/.config/Code/User). A directory that is relative, spelled as a
network or device path, or holds a control character is one coverage gap, and
nothing under it is read; the CLI verbs admit a plain UNC directory and follow
no link below it, while the MCP vet_configs tool refuses it. Under
\-\-evidence\-home the directory is the copied home's own, located by the
layout of the operating system Skarn runs on, and VSCODE_APPDATA is ignored.
\-\-project\-only reads none of these files. VS Code Insiders, VSCodium and
the other forks, the \-\-user\-data\-dir switch, portable mode, remote and
agent\-host settings are not read. Vet reads two settings and nothing else:
chat.tools.terminal.autoApprove and the deprecated
chat.agent.terminal.autoApprove, which VS Code still merges over it. The four
deprecated list settings beside them (chat.agent.terminal.allowList,
chat.agent.terminal.denyList and their github.copilot.chat.agent.terminal
twins) are read by nothing in VS Code and not by vet, and no other setting can
produce a finding. Comments, a byte\-order mark and a trailing comma after a
complete value are tolerated as VS Code tolerates them; a file that still does
not parse is a coverage gap. Every spelling VS Code honors is read: the dotted
key, nested objects, a mix of both, and a top\-level key that extends the
setting id, whose rest VS Code splits on every dot; a dotted key inside a
nested object is not a setting path, as in VS Code. Every occurrence of a
repeated setting is read. An entry's locator is the setting id with the
entry's ordinal in document order, which is not VS Code's last\-wins merge,
and the key text never enters a locator; a repeated setting, a repeated
ancestor object that holds the setting, a repeated rule key and a repeated
property inside a rule value are each reported as vet\-config\-duplicate\-key
with the setting id as its locator, and, unlike the other JSON files, a
repeated key anywhere else in the file is not reported. An empty or
comment\-only file is an empty configuration. Each key is un\-escaped from the
regular\-expression form in which VS Code saves an exact command line, and the
rule pack and the command\-line password detector check the command text
whatever the key's value: an approving entry, a deny (false), a null and
{approve: false} alike, each reported at most once as
vet\-permission\-plaintext\-credential at high. An approving key is reported
as vet\-permission\-overbroad at high only when it is a regular expression
whose pattern is on a closed list that matches every non\-empty single\-line
command (.*, ^, $, ., .+, ^., ^.+, ^.+$, .+$, [\es\eS], [\es\eS]+, [\eS\es],
[\ew\eW], [\ed\eD], [^], \eS, \eS+) with flags VS Code accepts, the sticky y
flag only on ^, whose empty match never advances the rule, and the detail is
the canonical pattern, such as /.*/. Any other pattern that matches the empty
string, such as /^.*$/, approves nothing in VS Code, which exempts only .*, ^,
$, ^$ and ^\es*$, and is not reported; a plain key is literal command text and
is never overbroad; a universal pattern outside the closed list is not
reported. Each profile directory's settings.json is read by the
walked\-directory rules: a symlinked profile directory is a coverage gap named
SymlinkNotFollowed, the walk stops at 64 files or 4 MiB, and a profile with no
settings file of its own, such as the built\-in agents profile, is skipped.
Stated limits: the command\-line password detector reads POSIX shell syntax,
so a PowerShell\-only quoting form can be missed. A settings file reached
under another reader's name (a symlinked or hard\-linked file, a Cline
override, a hook directory that resolves to a profile) is also read by that
generic reader, whose locator can carry a rule key in which nothing was
detected. On Windows vet masks a detected value in a path before it converts
forward slashes to backslashes for display, so a detected value that holds a
backslash can show in a path that spelled it with forward slashes. The user
settings file is opened before its type is checked, so a FIFO at that path can
block the read, and a network\-linked file is refused only where vet refuses
network links, as for every fixed\-path file.
.TP
\fB./.vscode/settings.json\fR
A workspace's VS Code settings, read by vet and by assess for the same two
terminal auto\-approve settings and by the same rules as the user settings
file above. Vet reads the file directly under each project directory it
visits: the working directory, the top of its git repository and, in a linked
worktree, the main checkout, and under \-\-all\-projects each recorded project
with its own git top and main checkout; \-\-project\-only reads it too. Vet
does not search folders below a visited directory, so a subfolder's file is
read only when vet starts in that subfolder, and the folder VS Code has open
is not known to vet. In a multi\-root workspace VS Code keeps workspace
settings in the .code\-workspace file, which vet does not read.
.TP
\fB~/Library/Application Support/Microsoft/CopilotAppHost/CopilotCodingHome/<profile>/mcp\-config.json, ~/Library/Application Support/Microsoft/CopilotAppHost/CopilotCodingHome/<profile>/config.json, ~/Library/Application Support/Microsoft/CopilotAppHost/CopilotCodingHome/<profile>/settings.json (macOS)\fR
The configuration files of one Copilot in Code profile on macOS, read by vet
when present, by the rules the Windows entry for these files states. On the
verified install a profile held config.json and held no mcp\-config.json or
settings.json. A home directory that is spelled as a network path or holds a
control character is a coverage gap, and vet opens nothing under it.
Read\-only; never written. Verified against Copilot app `158.0.4370.33`.
.TP
\fB~/Library/Application Support/Microsoft/CopilotAppHost/CopilotCodingHome/<profile>/data.db (macOS)\fR
The SQLite app database of one Copilot in Code profile on macOS; its settings
table has a github_access_token column. Vet reads this file's mode by a
non\-following stat and never opens it, as the Windows entry for this file
states, and reports a mode that grants group or other read. On the verified
install the mode was 0600. Verified against Copilot app `158.0.4370.33`.
.TP
\fB~/Library/Application Support/Microsoft/CopilotAppHost/CopilotCodingHome/<profile>/hooks/ (macOS)\fR
The hook folder of one Copilot in Code profile on macOS, read by vet when
present, as the Windows entry for this folder states. On the verified install
no profile held one. Verified against Copilot app `158.0.4370.33`.
.TP
\fB~/Library/Application Support/Code/User/globalStorage/saoudrizwan.claude\-dev/settings/cline_mcp_settings.json (macOS), ~/.config/Code/User/globalStorage/saoudrizwan.claude\-dev/settings/cline_mcp_settings.json (Linux), %APPDATA%\eCode\eUser\eglobalStorage\esaoudrizwan.claude\-dev\esettings\ecline_mcp_settings.json (Windows)\fR
The copy of Cline's MCP server definitions that the Cline extension wrote in
VS Code's user directory before it moved them to ~/.cline/data. The
extension's migration reads this copy and leaves it in place, so a server it
lists stays on disk after the move. Vet reads it as it reads
~/.cline/data/settings/cline_mcp_settings.json: flat servers and nested
transport objects, including nested env and headers, and each server's
autoApprove list; assess lists a credential in an autoApprove entry in its
saved permission rules section. ClineStateShape reports a root or an
mcpServers value that is not an object, and a file that does not parse is a
coverage gap. Vet inspects each path component before opening it, follows
local links, and refuses links to network shares; it reports SymlinkLoop after
eight links, NotARegularFile for a non\-regular file, and NetworkPathRefused
for a network target. ClineOverrideAlias reports a copy vet skips because it
is the same file as a credential store at its default location or as an
earlier Cline file, such as ~/.cline/data/settings/cline_mcp_settings.json; a
Cline override variable that names a copy's file is skipped instead, and the
copy is read. When this copy and the current file hold the same server, each
file reports its own findings. The directory follows VS Code's own rule:
$VSCODE_APPDATA/Code/User when VSCODE_APPDATA is set and not empty, on every
platform; otherwise %APPDATA%\eCode\eUser on Windows (falling back to
%USERPROFILE%\eAppData\eRoaming\eCode\eUser), ~/Library/Application
Support/Code/User on macOS, and $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/Code/User on Linux (falling
back to ~/.config/Code/User). A directory that is relative, spelled as a
network or device path, or holds a control character is one coverage gap, and
nothing under it is read; the CLI verbs admit a plain UNC directory and follow
no link below it, while the MCP vet_configs tool refuses it. Under
\-\-evidence\-home the directory is the copied home's own, located by the
layout of the operating system Skarn runs on, and VSCODE_APPDATA is ignored.
On Windows, when VSCODE_APPDATA, APPDATA and USERPROFILE are all unset or
empty, vet reads neither legacy copy and reports no gap. \-\-project\-only
reads neither legacy copy. VS Code Insiders, VSCodium, Windsurf and the other
forks are not read.
.TP
\fB~/Library/Application Support/Cursor/User/globalStorage/saoudrizwan.claude\-dev/settings/cline_mcp_settings.json (macOS), ~/.config/Cursor/User/globalStorage/saoudrizwan.claude\-dev/settings/cline_mcp_settings.json (Linux), %APPDATA%\eCursor\eUser\eglobalStorage\esaoudrizwan.claude\-dev\esettings\ecline_mcp_settings.json (Windows)\fR
The copy of Cline's MCP server definitions that the Cline extension wrote in
Cursor's user directory when it ran in Cursor, before it moved them to
~/.cline/data. The extension's migration reads this copy and leaves it in
place. Vet reads it as it reads
~/.cline/data/settings/cline_mcp_settings.json: flat servers and nested
transport objects, including nested env and headers, and each server's
autoApprove list; assess lists a credential in an autoApprove entry in its
saved permission rules section. ClineStateShape reports a root or an
mcpServers value that is not an object, and a file that does not parse is a
coverage gap. Vet inspects each path component before opening it, follows
local links, and refuses links to network shares; it reports SymlinkLoop after
eight links, NotARegularFile for a non\-regular file, and NetworkPathRefused
for a network target. ClineOverrideAlias reports a copy vet skips because it
is the same file as a credential store at its default location or as an
earlier Cline file, such as ~/.cline/data/settings/cline_mcp_settings.json; a
Cline override variable that names a copy's file is skipped instead, and the
copy is read. When this copy and the current file hold the same server, each
file reports its own findings. Cursor is built from VS Code, so the directory
follows VS Code's rule with Cursor in place of Code:
$VSCODE_APPDATA/Cursor/User when VSCODE_APPDATA is set and not empty, on every
platform; otherwise %APPDATA%\eCursor\eUser on Windows (falling back to
%USERPROFILE%\eAppData\eRoaming\eCursor\eUser), ~/Library/Application
Support/Cursor/User on macOS, and $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/Cursor/User on Linux
(falling back to ~/.config/Cursor/User). The VSCODE_APPDATA handling is
inherited from VS Code's source and has not been measured on a Cursor install.
A directory that is relative, spelled as a network or device path, or holds a
control character is one coverage gap, and nothing under it is read; the CLI
verbs admit a plain UNC directory and follow no link below it, while the MCP
vet_configs tool refuses it. Under \-\-evidence\-home the directory is the
copied home's own, located by the layout of the operating system Skarn runs
on, and VSCODE_APPDATA is ignored. On Windows, when VSCODE_APPDATA, APPDATA
and USERPROFILE are all unset or empty, vet reads neither legacy copy and
reports no gap. \-\-project\-only reads neither legacy copy.
.TP
\fB~/.cursor/cli\-config.json\fR
Cursor CLI permissions. Vet reads only permissions.allow, as grants, and
permissions.deny, as rules, and nothing else in the file, which also holds
account and cache fields. The CLI parses this file and the project file as
strict JSON, so vet reports a file with a comment, which the CLI rejects, as a
coverage gap. The Cursor CLI documents five entry forms, Shell(command),
Read(path), Write(path), WebFetch(domain) and Mcp(server:tool), and vet reads
each of them, and Bash(command) as the Shell alias the CLI also accepts,
trimming whitespace around the entry, before the opening parenthesis and
inside the parentheses as the CLI does, where whitespace is what JavaScript
counts as whitespace, the no\-break space included; an entry with a line break
or a Unicode line or paragraph separator inside its parentheses is not one the
CLI reads. In a Shell entry, the CLI splits the pattern at its first colon and
approves a command whose name or whole text matches the part before the colon
and whose arguments match the part after it, as well as a command that matches
the pattern as written. Vet runs the rule pack and the command\-line password
detector over two readings, each on its own: the command name, a space and the
arguments, as in mysql \-p<value> shop, and the stored text, as in
smbclient://host/share or a Basic authorization header, so a password one
reading would hide is still found. Vet takes the command name from the text
before the colon, up to its first star or whitespace, when only stars and
whitespace follow it there, so Shell(mysql *:\-p<value> shop) and
Shell(mysql*:\-p<value> shop) are both read as mysql \-p<value> shop; a star
inside the name, as in m*sql, leaves only the stored text. The command name
must not be empty, must have arguments after the colon, must not be a Windows
drive letter and must have no =, quote, parenthesis, $, backslash or shell
operator in it; any other entry is read only as stored, such as one with an
Authorization header after a command name, a URL argument or an environment
assignment. These allowed entries approve a whole class of actions and are
reported as vet\-permission\-overbroad, never when they are denied: a Shell
entry whose pattern starts with a star and holds only stars, whitespace and
colons, such as Shell(*), Shell(**) or Shell(*:*), with the detail Shell(*),
which includes the near\-blankets Shell(* *), which the CLI applies to every
command with an argument, and Shell(*:), which it applies to every command
without one; an Mcp entry whose server and tool parts are each only stars,
with the detail Mcp(*:*); WebFetch(*) and WebFetch([*]), whose brackets the
CLI removes; and a Read or Write entry whose path, after an optional leading
/, is star segments separated by single slashes, such as *, **, /** or */*,
with the detail Read(**) or Write(**), which includes a path such as /*/*/*
that the CLI applies to every file at that depth or deeper. A Read or Write
path that the CLI applies only to some paths, such as *:* to a path with a
colon, */ to a path that ends in a slash and *//* to a path with a double
slash, or to none, such as .* and ./**, which no absolute path matches, is not
a blanket. A Shell blanket is high and the other blankets medium, so the
pre\-commit hook's default \-\-fail\-on\-severity high fails only on a Shell
blanket. Any other string, such as a token the Cursor CLI may add later, an
undocumented wildcard form such as Mcp(*), or a bare *, ** or *:* entry, which
the CLI ignores because it has no wrapper, is still checked for a credential,
as a rule that is never reported as overbroad, and raises no coverage gap, so
a new Cursor entry form does not stop the pre\-commit hook or the GitHub
Action. A value that is not a string, a list that is not an array and a
permissions value that is not an object are coverage gaps named by the entry's
index or the list, never by its content, and the entries beside them are still
read; one gap budget covers both readings of a file that repeats a key. A
repeated key is read both ways and reported as vet\-config\-duplicate\-key at
the file, never by the key's name. In the one case measured, a curl command
with an Authorization header on cursor\-agent 2026.11.22, the CLI's
always\-allow choice saved only Shell(curl), without the header; other
commands can be saved with their arguments. CURSOR_CONFIG_DIR and
XDG_CONFIG_HOME are not followed.
.TP
\fB./.cursor/cli.json\fR
Cursor CLI project permissions, read by vet at the working directory, the git
top and, in a linked worktree, the main checkout with the same reader and the
same rules as ~/.cursor/cli\-config.json, whether or not the workspace is
trusted, and reported apart from the home file. The CLI also reads and merges
a .cursor/cli.json in each directory between the git root and the working
directory; vet reads neither those directories nor a .cursor/cli.json in a
subdirectory below the working directory. When the file is committed with the
repository, an entry that carries a credential reaches every clone; the
pre\-commit hook, and the GitHub Action when its vet input is true, read it
through \-\-project\-only. Assess reads it in the working directory's project
and in every project Claude Code records.
.TP
\fB~/.gemini/policies/\fR
Gemini CLI's user policy files. When GEMINI_CLI_HOME is nonempty, vet reads
$GEMINI_CLI_HOME/.gemini/policies/ instead. Vet skips a missing directory
silently. If vet cannot open an existing directory, it reports the error as a
coverage gap. Vet lists only direct children. If the directory has more than
4096 entries, vet reports RuleDirEntryBudgetExceeded before reading any file.
It sorts names ending in .toml in byte order and checks the first 64,
reporting RuleFileCountBudgetExceeded if there are more. A matching symbolic
link or other entry that is not a regular file produces NotARegularFile, also
below an evidence home or a network root. Gemini CLI itself skips linked
files. Vet skips a file that aliases a protected credential store, or that vet
cannot distinguish from such a store, and reports ProtectedStoreAlias. Vet
reads up to 4 MiB of policy content in total. A file over 4 MiB is reported as
StreamTooLong and the walk continues. A smaller file that exceeds the bytes
left in the budget stops the scan of the directory with
RuleDirByteBudgetExceeded. Vet also parses at most 65,536 values in the
directory, counting every table header, every Codex CLI call, and every
string, number, boolean, date, list and inline table: the file in which the
count passes that limit reports RuleDirValueBudgetExceeded, keeps the findings
of what vet read before that point, and vet reads no later file in the
directory. A file that holds fewer bytes when read than its size when opened,
because it shrank during the read, produces FileShrankDuringRead and
contributes nothing. Vet checks a file against the protected credential stores
before it applies the budget. Vet reads [[rule]] and [[safety_checker]]
tables, and a [safety_checker.checker] table that follows its safety checker,
the form Gemini CLI's own policies use. An allow decision creates a grant. An
ask_user or deny decision creates a rule and can still produce a high
vet\-permission\-plaintext\-credential finding. Vet checks each commandPrefix
string as run_shell_command(prefix). For commandRegex and argsPattern, vet
removes a leading caret, an unescaped trailing dollar sign, and backslashes
before punctuation. It checks the commandRegex text as
run_shell_command(text). Gemini matches argsPattern against a tool's JSON
arguments, so vet checks that text as written, without the run_shell_command
wrapper; the command\-line password detector sees it only when the pattern
itself spells a shell tool call such as Shell(command). A safety checker
approves nothing. Vet checks its command fields and the strings inside its
checker and toolAnnotations tables for credentials. Vet reports a high
vet\-permission\-overbroad finding with the detail run_shell_command(*) when a
valid allow rule names run_shell_command or * in toolName, either directly or
in an array, supplies no mcpName or toolAnnotations, and either supplies no
commandPrefix, commandRegex or argsPattern or has a commandRegex or
argsPattern that, after vet removes a leading caret and an unescaped trailing
dollar sign, is one of .*, .+, .*?, .+?, [\es\eS]*, [\es\eS]+, [\eS\es]*,
[\eS\es]+, [^]*, [^]+, (.*), (.+), (?:.*) or (?:.+). An empty string in any of
the three command fields imposes no restriction, and an empty element of a
commandPrefix array matches every command that starts with whitespace, so vet
reports either as the blanket. Gemini treats a prefix of *, ** or *:* as
literal text, so vet never reports that prefix as a blanket grant. Gemini CLI
offers "Allow this command for all future sessions" only when
security.enablePermanentToolApproval is true. In Gemini CLI 0.71.1, that
choice saved only the command name. A credential in a command argument reaches
the policy file through a manually written entry. Vet reports unsupported
syntax at line N for a value of the wrong type, an empty toolName or
commandPrefix array, a repeated or dotted key, a key before the first table,
or a table header other than [[rule]] or [[safety_checker]], and then
continues with the next table. When vet cannot parse the TOML text at a line,
for example an unclosed value, a malformed header line, a malformed number,
date or line ending, a control character or an escape outside the TOML escapes
in a string, an array that mixes value types, or a repeated or conflicting
inline table member, it reports that line and reads nothing after it, because
Gemini CLI loads nothing from a file that fails to parse. The tables above
that line keep their findings. A rule requires toolName, decision, and
priority. A safety checker requires toolName and checker. A missing required
field produces the same gap. A rule with commandPrefix or commandRegex
requires toolName to be the single string run_shell_command. Either field
alongside argsPattern, both fields together, or either with another toolName
form produces a gap. Vet still checks the command fields in these conflicting
combinations for credentials. Vet reports up to 16 syntax gaps per file, then
one overflow gap, unsupported syntax on more than 16 lines. Vet checks up to
4096 entries per file and reports artifact budget exceeded at the next entry.
A coverage gap makes vet exit 6 under \-\-fail\-on\-scan\-error. An otherwise
valid rule with an unknown decision or mode, a numeric priority outside 0 to
999 or with a fractional part, or an empty toolName, mcpName, or subagent
string produces no gap. Vet checks its command fields for credentials and
skips overbroad scoring. In a toolName array, an empty string leaves the other
names in effect. Gemini ignores unknown keys. Vet checks every string under an
unknown key or under toolAnnotations for credentials and omits the key name
from the report. When masking cannot settle on a safe spelling of a policy
file's path, vet prints [path withheld] in its place and assess withholds the
location. Gemini CLI disables workspace policies. Vet skips workspace
\&.gemini/policies/, system policy directories, and paths in
settings.policyPaths. Assess lists a credential from a user policy under
Credentials in saved permission rules at gemini home policies/ followed by the
file name.
.TP
\fB~/.codex/rules/\fR
Codex CLI's user rules files. When CODEX_HOME is nonempty, vet reads
$CODEX_HOME/rules/ instead. Codex CLI writes a command the user approves with
Yes, and don't ask again for commands that start with a prefix to
default.rules here, as a prefix_rule call holding the command's tokens.
Measured on codex\-cli 0.172.0 with two such approvals, a curl command
carrying a bearer header and a command that starts with a PGPASSWORD=
assignment: each stored call held every token of the command, the credential
included, and the command that starts with the assignment was stored as the
shell argv that ran it, /bin/zsh \-lc followed by the whole command line. Vet
walks the directory as it walks Gemini CLI's policies/ directory, with the
same budgets and the same coverage gaps, reading names that end in .rules;
Codex CLI itself skips a linked file, which vet reports as NotARegularFile.
Codex CLI evaluates these files as Starlark. Vet evaluates nothing: it reads
top\-level calls whose arguments are literal strings, numbers in the spellings
Codex CLI accepts, True, False, None and lists of them nested at most 64 deep,
given by keyword or by position in Codex CLI's order. A prefix_rule call with
decision allow, or with no decision, creates a grant. A prompt or forbidden
call creates a rule and can still produce a high
vet\-permission\-plaintext\-credential finding. Vet joins the pattern's tokens
with single spaces, quotes a token that is empty or holds whitespace, a quote,
a shell metacharacter or a glob character, and checks the result as
exec_command(command line), so the rule pack and the command\-line password
detector see the command line the user approved. Codex CLI treats every token
as literal text, so a * token never makes a grant overbroad. A list of
alternatives inside the pattern expands to one entry per combination, up to 16
per call, reported as prefix_rule[n].pattern[k]; a prefix_rule call with no
alternatives is reported as prefix_rule[n]. n counts every prefix_rule call in
file order, including one vet rejects, so a rejected call never shifts the
index of a later one. Vet checks the type of the match, not_match,
justification and canonical_matching values and then skips them, and does the
same for host_executable and network_rule calls, which approve no command
line. A call that Codex CLI rejects but vet can read raises no coverage gap:
an unknown decision, an unknown keyword with a literal value, or an empty or
whitespace\-only justification makes vet check the call's patterns as rules
that are never overbroad. So does a string escape that vet does not decode,
such as \ea, \e0 or \eU: Codex CLI accepts those, and vet keeps the escape as
written. A backslash before a line break inside a string continues the string,
as Starlark reads it. Vet also checks every string under an unknown keyword,
and every string in a top\-level call of another name, reported at call[n],
and prints neither the keyword nor the call's name. Vet reports unsupported
syntax at line N, and reads nothing from that call, for a value of the wrong
type, a missing or empty pattern, an empty or nested alternatives list, more
than 16 combinations, a repeated keyword, a positional argument after a
keyword, more positional arguments than the call takes, a number Codex CLI
rejects such as 007 or 1e+, a list nested more than 64 deep, a variable, an
expression, a raw, byte, f\- or triple\-quoted string, or a statement that is
not a call; it then reads the next statement. An unclosed string, call or
list, a line break inside a single\-quoted string, a closing bracket that does
not match, or brackets nested more than 256 deep is one gap at the statement's
first line, and vet reads nothing after it. Codex CLI applies no user or
project rule at all when any rules file fails to load, so a gap or an unknown
form in one file can mean that none of the rules is in force. Vet reports up
to 16 syntax gaps per file, then unsupported syntax on more than 16 lines, and
checks up to 4096 entries per file, reporting artifact budget exceeded at the
next entry. It also builds at most 16 MiB of command text per file, so a call
whose alternatives would repeat a long literal past that reports artifact
budget exceeded too. Assess lists a credential from a user rules file at codex
home rules/ followed by the file name.
.TP
\fB./.codex/rules/\fR
Codex CLI project rules files, read by vet at the working directory, the git
top and, in a linked worktree, the main checkout with the same reader and the
same rules as ~/.codex/rules/, and reported apart from the home files. Codex
CLI loads them only in a project the user marked trusted; vet reads them
whenever they are present. A directory reached through both this row and the
home row, such as ~/.codex/rules/ when vet runs in the home directory or a
project's .codex/rules/ when CODEX_HOME names that project's .codex, is read
once, under the home row; with \-\-project\-only there is no home row and it
is read under this one. When the files are committed with the repository, an
entry that carries a credential reaches every clone; the pre\-commit hook, and
the GitHub Action when its vet input is true, read them through
\-\-project\-only. Assess reads them in the working directory's project and in
every project Claude Code records.
